{"name":"Devlan Mud Wash","hex":"#3c3523","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mud","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Mednyj Aleut."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mixture of water and soil or fine grained sediment.","A plaster-like mixture used to texture or smooth drywall."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mud"},{"term":"wash","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To clean with water.","To carry away or erode by the force of water in motion."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The process or an instance of washing or being washed by water or other liquid.","A liquid used for washing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wash"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mud","blocks":[{"text":"Mud is loam, silt, or clay mixed with water. Mud is usually formed after rainfall or near water sources. Ancient mud deposits hardened over geological time to form sedimentary rock such as shale or mudstone. When geological deposits of mud are formed in estuaries, the resultant layers are termed bay muds. Mud has also been used for centuries as a construction resource, mostly for houses, and also as a binder. An Old English word for it was fen, now in most dialects referring to a type of wetland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/2003-11-27_Northerner_boots_in_mud.jpg/330px-2003-11-27_Northerner_boots_in_mud.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mud","wikidata":"Q170449"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Gamo_Mud_Volcano_3.jpg/500px-Gamo_Mud_Volcano_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Gamo_Mud_Volcano_3.jpg/1280px-Gamo_Mud_Volcano_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi